.\" teachatty(1): the client. Plain man(7) macros, no build step: read it with man ./man/teachatty.1
.TH TEACHATTY 1 "2026-10-12" "teachatty 0.1.0" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
teachatty \- a minimalist terminal chat
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B teachatty
.RB [ \-\-server
.IR host [: port ]]
.RB [ \-\-room
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-alias
.IR name ]
.RB [ \-\-no\-tls ]
.br
.I command
.B |
.B teachatty
.RI [ flags ]
.br
.B teachatty
.RB [ \-\-version " | " \-h " | " help ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B teachatty
joins a chat room on a
.BR teachatty\-server (1)
and lets you talk with everyone in it, from the terminal.
.PP
When standard input is a terminal, it opens a full\-screen chat: messages on top,
an input box at the bottom that grows as you type. Incoming messages never overwrite
what you are typing. Each alias gets its own color, the same on every client, and
your own messages show your alias in bold.
.PP
When standard input is not a terminal (\fIcommand\fR | teachatty), it runs in
\fBpipe mode\fR: each input line is sent to the room and echoed to standard output,
like
.BR tee (1).
Lines that arrive within 100 ms of each other go to the room as one message, so a
long build log doesn't flood it. Empty lines are echoed but not sent. Pipe mode never
opens the screen and exits when the input ends, so it is safe in scripts and
.BR cron (8).
.PP
A room exists while someone is in it: joining a room that doesn't exist creates it.
The default room,
.BR local ,
always exists, so everyone who skips
.B \-\-room
meets there. There is no history: a message only reaches whoever is in the room
when it is sent.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-\-server " host\fR[:\fIport\fR]"
The server to connect to. Without a port, teachatty's port 7347 is used.
Default:
.BR localhost:7347 .
.TP
.BI \-\-room " name"
The room to join. It is created if it doesn't exist. Default:
.BR local .
.TP
.BI \-\-alias " name"
The name shown in front of your messages. Default: your login
.RB ( $USER ),
or
.B anonymous
when it is unset.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-tls
Connect over plain ws://, without encryption, to a server started with
.BR \-\-no\-tls .
.TP
.B \-\-version
Print the version and exit.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help ", " help
Print the help and exit.
.SH KEYS
.TP
.B Enter
Send the message.
.TP
.BR PgUp ", " PgDn
Scroll through the messages. Typing anything jumps back to the newest.
.TP
.B Ctrl+C
Leave.
.SH MENTIONS
Write
.BI @ name
to mention someone: the message shows in amber and bold on their screen and their
terminal bell rings, so a terminal in the background gets their attention. The match
is a whole word, in any case.
.SH SECURITY
Connections use TLS (wss://) by default. A certificate signed by an authority this
machine trusts (Let's Encrypt, an installed company CA) is verified like a browser
does. A self\-signed certificate is trusted the first time you connect: its
fingerprint is saved in
.IR ~/.config/teachatty/known_servers ,
and if it changes later the client refuses to connect, like
.BR ssh (1).
Compare the fingerprint the client shows on first use with the one the server prints.
.PP
Aliases are not verified, and rooms have no passwords: anyone who knows a room's
name can join it.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Left with Ctrl+C, or pipe mode sent all of its input.
.TP
.B 1
The connection could not be made or dropped, the server refused it (too many
connections from your address, or pipe mode on a server started with
.BR \-\-no\-pipes ),
or the server's certificate changed.
.TP
.B 2
A stray argument or an unknown flag; the help is printed.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.config/teachatty/known_servers
Fingerprints of the self\-signed servers you trust, one per line:
.IR host : port " " fingerprint .
Delete a line to trust a server again after its certificate legitimately changed.
.SH EXAMPLES
Join the public server's lobby:
.PP
.RS
teachatty \-\-server teachatty.com
.RE
.PP
Make a room for your friends; they run the same command:
.PP
.RS
teachatty \-\-server teachatty.com \-\-room tea\-with\-friends
.RE
.PP
Join a server on your network as \fBana\fR:
.PP
.RS
teachatty \-\-server 192.168.0.10 \-\-room dev \-\-alias ana
.RE
.PP
Send a build's output to the room:
.PP
.RS
make 2>&1 | teachatty \-\-server chat.example.com \-\-room ops
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR teachatty\-server (1)
.PP
https://teachatty.com/docs.html
.SH BUGS
Report bugs at https://github.com/daniel\-cmdline/teachatty/issues.
